{
 "id": "2026-11-10-escapade-earth-flyby",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Two NASA probes just dove at Earth on purpose. Why the detour to Mars?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "NASA's twin ESCAPADE probes, named Blue and Gold, launched in November twenty twenty-five on a Blue Origin rocket. Earth and Mars were badly placed then, so the probes waited near Earth for a full year."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "On November seventh, Blue fired its engine for more than nine minutes while passing close to Earth, Berkeley News reports. Gold did the same two days later. Burning deep in Earth's gravity adds extra speed. Rocket Lab, which built the probes, says both burns were completed."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "NASA had said no Mars-bound spacecraft had done this kind of powered flyby before. Both probes are now headed for Mars, with arrival planned for September twenty twenty-seven, to study how the solar wind interacts with the planet."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will both probes reach Mars on schedule? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
